        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing and Certifications
          </h2>

          <p>
            Testing is where Ritual earns its price, so it is worth being precise about what is claimed. Ritual's current Daily Shake and Pregnancy and Postpartum pages display a Clean Label Project Purity Award and NSF Contents Certification. The Clean Label Project screens products for contaminants such as heavy metals and some chemical residues. NSF Contents Certification checks that what is in the product matches what the label says. Ritual also says it tests for heavy metals and microbes and lists the shake as tested free of major allergens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Sport is the complicated one. Ritual published an article announcing that its Essential Protein line was Informed Sport certified, and the Informed Sport registry has listed the 18+, 50+ and Pregnancy and Postpartum products with certification dates in spring 2021 and batch records after that. The current product pages we checked no longer show the Informed Sport mark. If you are a drug-tested athlete, look up your specific lot number on the Informed Sport site before you rely on it, rather than trusting any product page, including this one.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth knowing what these marks do not mean. NSF Contents Certification is not the same program as NSF Certified for Sport, which is the banned-substance standard most professional leagues reference. And no certification makes a product organic: Ritual itself says the shake combines organic and non-organic ingredients. The chocolate page also lists Non-GMO Project verification, which is a farming claim, not a purity claim.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHY IT MATTERS FOR PEA PROTEIN</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Plant proteins carry a higher average heavy-metal risk than whey.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Peas pull minerals, including lead and cadmium, from soil, so contaminant screening matters more for a pea shake than for most dairy proteins. That is the strongest argument for paying extra for a pea protein with independent purity testing, and it is the main thing Ritual is selling.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For context on how worried to be about contaminants in general, our guide to whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> walks through what the testing data shows and what it does not. The short version is that documented testing does reduce your risk, and Ritual documents more than most plant brands do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Skip Ritual
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Ritual if you are trying to build muscle on a budget. Lifters who use two shakes a day to reach a target of 140 g or more of protein will spend a great deal on Ritual compared with a standard whey or pea isolate, and they will need more scoops to get there because each serving holds 20 g. You can buy the same protein, and more of it per scoop, for a fraction of the cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are a drug-tested athlete who cannot confirm a current certified lot. Ritual's Informed Sport history is real, but the mark no longer appears on the product pages we checked. If your sport requires certified products, a powder that currently carries NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport on the label is the safer buy. Garden of Life Sport, in the lineup above, is one example that the brand and NSF list as certified.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you dislike stevia and only want vanilla, because the vanilla shake uses rebaudioside M. Skip it if you are watching sodium closely, since the standard shake carries about 290 mg per serving. And skip it if you want a powder for baking or cooking: a flavored, sweetened pea shake is the wrong tool, and an unflavored isolate will do the job better at a lower price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, skip it if you are fine with dairy and simply want the cleanest possible protein. Whey isolate tends to test lower for heavy metals than pea protein, delivers more leucine per gram, and certified whey isolates like Thorne's typically cost less per 25 g than Ritual does at list prices. Ritual makes the most sense for people who need, or strongly prefer, a dairy-free shake.
          </p>
        </div>

