        <div id="using" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Getting the Most From a Ritual Shake
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because each serving holds 20 g, the simplest upgrade is to build the rest of the dose around it. Mixing the shake with a cup of soy milk adds roughly 7 g of protein, and dairy milk adds about 8 g, which takes you comfortably past 25 g. Blending it with Greek yogurt does the same and thickens the texture. This matters most after training, where a slightly larger dose tends to be more useful than a small one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buyer feedback on mixing is generally positive for a pea protein, with the usual caveat that pea shakes thicken as they sit. A shaker bottle works, but a blender gives a smoother result, and drinking it soon after mixing helps. If you find the vanilla too sweet or notice an aftertaste, the chocolate flavor uses monk fruit without stevia and is the obvious thing to try next.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about how many servings you actually need. A 15-serving bag lasts two weeks at one shake a day, which makes the subscription price the only sensible way to buy it regularly. If you only need protein on training days, a bag might last a month, and Ritual's cost per gram becomes easier to absorb. If you want two shakes a day, the cost doubles and the case for a cheaper powder becomes overwhelming.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep the bag sealed and cool once opened. Pea protein is stable, but the MCT oil and flavorings fare better away from heat and humidity, and a clumpy bag is harder to mix. Check the best-by date if you are buying from a marketplace seller, especially for any older 18+ or 50+ listings that may have been sitting in a warehouse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing price per bag instead of per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ritual's bag looks similar in price to many tubs, but it holds 15 servings of 20 g. Always convert to cost per 25 g of protein before comparing. On that basis Ritual typically costs two to three times as much as a mainstream pea isolate, which is the real decision you are making.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming it is USDA Organic</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The pea protein is organic, but Ritual's own pages say the finished product combines organic and non-organic ingredients and is not organic as a whole. If certified organic matters to you, look for the USDA seal on the bag rather than inferring it from the word organic in the ingredient list.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating NSF Contents as NSF Certified for Sport</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                They are different programs. NSF Contents Certification confirms the label matches what is inside, while NSF Certified for Sport screens specifically for banned substances. Tested athletes should look up their exact lot in a sport certification database rather than relying on any badge on a product page.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stopping at one 20 g scoop after training</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single Ritual serving sits below the 25 to 30 g dose many lifters aim for in one meal. Mix it with milk, soy milk or Greek yogurt, or eat protein alongside it, so the shake contributes to an adequate dose instead of falling short of one.
              </p>
